        "pkcontributionId": 62995,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "<p>Peritoneum, pelvic or abdominal organs, skin, nerves, and lymph nodes {29739266; 19020998; 31122517; 27841144; 23249836; 20631604}</p>",
        "timestamp": "2020-06-19T16:06:44"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62996,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It is usually an incidental finding {31033796; 31122517; 19020998}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:21:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62997,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It occurs in as many as 12.5% of women, with a wide age range {27261327}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:21:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62998,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:21:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62999,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The pathogenesis is unknown. Endosalpingiosis of the peritoneum has been suggested to originate through metaplastic transformation, direct implantation of exfoliated tubal epithelium, or transit via lymphatics {31033796}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:21:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63000,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>It usually occurs as multiple small nodules or cysts {31122517}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:21:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 63001,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 158,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Endosalpingiosis is lined by a single layer of tubal-like epithelium, sometimes associated with psammoma bodies. Small papillae or epithelial proliferation without cell detachment can be seen {31033796}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-19T15:21:01"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
